From: SwiftPili <359733720+haikiseven@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] es_out: the master source owns the program PCR

BiliBili serves DASH as separate audio and video files. The video file is the
input master; the audio track is attached as a slave via
libvlc_media_add_slave(). Both demuxers feed ES_OUT_SET_PCR into the one program
input_clock:

  * es_out_source.c EsOutSourceControl() forwards the slave's control (including
    ES_OUT_SET_PCR) to the main es_out, tagged with the slave source.
  * EsOutVaControlLocked() / case ES_OUT_SET_PCR applies it to p_sys->p_pgrm --
    the master program's input_clock -- regardless of which source sent it. It
    updates p_pgrm->i_last_pcr and calls input_clock_Update().

In steady state master and slave PCRs are close (diff < CR_MAX_GAP), so this is
harmless. But at an in-place set_time seek the two demuxers snap to different
fragment boundaries: the video lands on the 5.000s grid, the audio on an
AAC-frame-aligned boundary a few ms later (documented in patch 0003). The slave
PCR then perturbs the shared program clock, and the input_clock logs the
signature triple at every seek:

  clock gap ... stream_diff:  2781000   (+2.78s)  video master fragment
  clock gap ... stream_diff: -2531000   (-2.53s)  audio slave fragment (earlier)
  clock gap ... stream_diff:  2516000   (+2.52s)  back to master

followed by chronic "picture is too late to be displayed (missing 20-59 ms)" and
eventually "resetting master clock: coefficient too unstable: 0.708333". The
clock never re-stabilises, so video sits 1-2 frames late and the audio runs
ahead of the video -- permanently, with no self-correction.

reopenForSeek (the historical VOD default) never hits this: :start-time opens
both demuxers fresh at the target, so there is no in-place SlaveSeek and no
fragment-boundary bounce. Patch 0003 already aligns the slave to the requested
time, so the seek itself is clean, but the two demuxers still land on different
fragment starts and both still drive the shared clock.

Fix: the master (video) source owns the program PCR timeline. In case
ES_OUT_SET_PCR, drop the PCR when it comes from a non-master source -- return
VLC_SUCCESS without updating i_last_pcr or calling input_clock_Update(). This
mirrors the master/slave idiom already used in this file (EsOutIsGroupSticky:
source != input_priv(p_sys->p_input)->master).

Why this is safe / narrowly scoped:
  * Muxed single-file A+V is unaffected: one source (the master) emits PCR, so
    the guard never trips.
  * Only the DASH separate-slave case changes -- exactly the target.
  * Buffering (EsOutDecodersStopBuffering) is driven by the master input_clock,
    which the master demux still feeds regularly -- unaffected.
  * The i_last_pcr fallback for undated blocks is irrelevant to audio: AAC slave
    blocks carry PTS.
  * ES data still flows: EsOutSourceSend() is untouched -- only the slave's clock
    control is dropped.
  * At a seek es_out already sets b_buffering + input_clock_Reset
    (b_has_reference=false), so the master's first post-seek PCR resets cleanly
    via the !b_has_reference path -- one reference, aligned A/V, no gap warning.

ES_OUT_SET_GROUP_PCR (explicit multi-program muxes, not the slave case) is left
untouched by guarding on i_query == ES_OUT_SET_PCR.

diff --git a/src/input/es_out.c b/src/input/es_out.c
index d341adac71..0641d36f44 100644
--- a/src/input/es_out.c
+++ b/src/input/es_out.c
@@ -3551,12 +3551,29 @@ static int EsOutVaControlLocked(es_out_sys_t *p_sys, input_source_t *source,
         if( i_pcr == VLC_TICK_INVALID )
         {
             msg_Err( p_sys->p_input, "Invalid PCR value in ES_OUT_SET_(GROUP_)PCR !" );
             return VLC_EGENERIC;
         }

+        /* SwiftVLC 0008: the master source owns the program PCR timeline.
+         * BiliBili DASH serves audio as a slave demuxer (libvlc_media_add_slave);
+         * its ES_OUT_SET_PCR routes here (es_out_source.c) tagged with the slave
+         * source but is applied to p_sys->p_pgrm, the MASTER program's clock. In
+         * steady state master/slave PCRs are close and harmless, but at a set_time
+         * seek the two demuxers snap to different fragment boundaries (video on the
+         * 5.000s grid, audio AAC-frame-aligned -- see patch 0003). The slave PCR
+         * then perturbs the shared program input_clock, destabilising the master
+         * clock coefficient, and video sits 1-2 frames late -> audio ahead of
+         * video, permanently. Drop non-master PCR: the master (video) demux drives
+         * the clock. Mirrors the master/slave idiom used elsewhere in this file
+         * (EsOutIsGroupSticky). ES_OUT_SET_GROUP_PCR (explicit multi-program
+         * muxes) is unaffected. */
+        if( i_query == ES_OUT_SET_PCR &&
+            source != input_priv(p_sys->p_input)->master )
+            return VLC_SUCCESS;
+
         p_pgrm->i_last_pcr = i_pcr;

         struct vlc_tracer *tracer = vlc_object_get_tracer( &p_sys->p_input->obj );
         if ( tracer != NULL )
         {
             vlc_tracer_TracePCR(tracer, "DEMUX", "PCR", i_pcr);
